lem as well money the world's  richer nations had promised to pay the  poorest and most vulnerable 100 billion  dollars a year by now to compensate for  the damage global warming was doing and  to help move them toward green  technologies  now that promise has been put off too  for two more years  and as you know we and small islands  have used the slogan 1.5 to stay alive  how disappointed are you  that the money that  countries like yours were promised  in paris is still  not been delivered  well we are very disappointed because  it's a promise  made a promise failed you want them to  show you the money  that too yes  a climate think tank in princeton new  jersey has produced a grim little movie  showing what science says will be the  result of three degrees of warming  contrasted with what would happen if  greenhouse gases are reduced  for instance south florida can't be  defended miami will be gone one day but  if that happens more slowly  fewer people will lose money fewer  people will lose livelihoods will be  able to manage it  so where do we stand now negotiators  have been up all night producing yet  another final draft for this conference  it still has to be approved  it does provide for more of a prospect  for more money for poorer countries and  about the end of the use of fossil fuels  particularly coal but does it get us to  that magic 1.5 degree celsius figure  beyond which global heating would become  critical  not yet  for cbs saturday morning i'm mark  phillips in glasgow  oh  mark always great to see mark by the way  so thank you for that yeah i mean and he  touched on this that one of the big  disputes here is that between rich and  poor nations and his poor nation's  saying you can afford to do this we  can't and also you've promised a certain  amount of money and haven't delivered it  right and i think people might think  well a few degrees what difference does  that make right but a scientist  explained to me when the body the human  body has 